

                        MAKEOVER 1.20
                        24 November 1997


IMPORTANT NOTICE
================
Please note that, although MAKEOVER has been placed in the public domain, 
any overlay files you convert with it may contain copyrighted data. 
Such data remains copyrighted even if converted.
Specifically, the overlay files that are distributed as part of the 
En Route products (on CD-Rom or Solid State Disk) contain copyrighted 
material and may not be distributed in any form, nor used for any purpose 
except for private use by the owner of the En Route product.



USING MAKEOVER.EXE
==================

The DOS-application MAKEOVER can convert between En Route overlays and 
simple text files.

Palmtop overlay files are files with extension OVR. They can be created 
or maintained with versions of En Route running on a Psion Series 3a/3c, 
a Psion Series 5, on a Geofox One, or on any other EPOC-compatible system. 

After you have copied an overlay file SOMEFILE.OVR from your palmtop to 
your PC, you can convert it to an editable text file called MYFILE.ASC, 
by typing 

	MAKEOVER SOMEFILE.OVR MYFILE.ASC 

You can also write a textfile from scratch (see next section). 

The resulting text file can be edited using any text editor. 
(If you use a rich text editor, like Word for Windows, take care that your 
document is saved as text-only). 

You can then convert it back into an overlay file called, say, NEWFILE.OVR, 
by typing 

	MAKEOVER MYFILE.ASC NEWFILE.OVR 

You can also simply type 

	MAKEOVER 

(i.e. without any arguments) and enter a very simple interactive shell. 



2. Writing an overlay text file from scratch 
============================================

	Note: We respectfully request anyone who writes applications 
	that can IMPORT our formats to also offer options to EXPORT 
	to our formats. 

MAKEOVER simply scans a text file for lines that it recognizes: 
a longitude, a latitude, and a name, separated by comma's. 
The name may be anything from 0 to 125 characters long, and MAY 
be placed between quotes ("). 

Empty lines, and lines starting with a semi-colon (;) will be ignored. 
Spaces (except inside strings) are ignored. 

Note that longitude and latitude may be as precise as you want. 
The current version of MAKEOVER will round it off to 4 decimals, 
since that is the precision of the current versions of En Route. 
However, future versions will support more decimals. 

So, a valid text file could be: 

	; our office: 
	4.7056 , 52.2989 , "Palmtop Headquarters" 

You can also specify coordinates in degrees, minutes and seconds. 
So instead of a longitude of 4.7056 degrees, you could also specify 
4 degrees 42 minutes and 20 seconds. You would write this with 
colons (:) as separator, i.e. the following would also be correct: 

	; our office: 
	4:42:20 , 52:17:56 , Palmtop Headquarters 

Furthermore, after such a record, one or more lines of the following 
format may follow: 

	nr = "string" 

where nr may be 2, 3, 4, 5 or 6, and string may be up to 125 characters, 
and MAY be between quotes ("). Example: 

	; my office: 
	4.7056, 52.2989, "Palmtop Headquarters" 
		2=Zandsteen 54 
		3=Amsterdam 
		4="Email: info@palmtop.nl" 

Please note that MAKEOVER will build an overlay containing the 
numbered fields, but that the current versions of En Route will 
ignore this information.


3. Distributing overlay files via our website 
==============================================
Palmtop BV will set up web pages to distribute overlays. If you have 
data (either as an OVR file or as a text file) which you think would 
interest others, you can submit it by E-mail to 

	overlays@palmtop.nl 

Please specify: 
- The COUNTRY (or set of countries) for which the data is suitable. 
- A brief description of the data. 
- Your name and/or E-mail address and/or other contact information, 
  and any personal copyrights to your data you may wish to include. 

The files you submit to us will be put in the public domain via our 
website. We will not distribute your data in any other form or use 
it for commercial purposes. 

Please make sure you have the legal right to submit your data to us, 
either because you collected it yourself or because the data is in 
the public domain to begin with. In other words, please don't type 
in the 1998 Michelin guide and put it on our webpages. 

